As long as nothing is pulling in non-js files with `require()`.  I know for starters that enketo pulls in at least two `.json` files.  We override both of those in our browserify config, so it wouldn't actually be a problem in that case, but:

@garethbowen @mandric I haven't measured, but I think the performance implications of watching extra files & dirs is probably negligible considering how long it actually takes to (1) re-browserify the entire JS stack, including node-modules, and (2) re-kanso push.  On my machine, **1** can often take 15+ seconds, and **2** can take 30.

So on balance, I think it's safer to stick with `node_modules/**/*`, as the performance boost from specifying `*.js` is likely negligible, and there's potential for weird behaviour that would be very hard to diagnose.

So the entire original code (without my var renaming) is this: 
```js
var docId = doc[f];
if (typeof docId === 'object') {
  docId = doc[f]._id;
}
return DB().get(doc[f])
  .then(function(dbFieldValue) {
    doc[f] = dbFieldValue;
    return doc;
  });
```

So `docId === doc[f]`, and `docId` can be an object sometimes. That would then mean `DB().get(doc[f])` was sometimes passing an object to get. More importantly, `docId` is never actually used after its set. I presume it's *supposed* to read `return DB().get(docId)`.

So to me that either means:
 - `doc[f]` can be a string id but *never* be an object, and we replace that id with an object sometimes (I'd love an example of this, because if I could get rid of it I could keep the code synchronous :P)
 - the code is never called at all

The options for `doc[f]` in that entire if statement block are:
 - undefined, in which case it becomes `{}` (why!?!?)
 - `"NEW"`, in which case we attach an "extra" / sibling we absolutely must find at that location
 - an id which is the same as the original document, in which case we just use the attached object from the original
 - anything else, in which case if it's a string we attempt to load that object and attach it, and otherwise (IMO, see above) we crash

I understand and know examples of the first two, but the last 2-3 are mysteries to me.

